from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-3867';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2, protocols and enforceability; Section 3, unweighted discrete-concave costs; Section 5, Definition 9, Theorem 10 and the following remark on p. 154:12.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Efficient Black-Box Reductions for Separable Cost Sharing',authors='Tobias Harks; Martin Hoefer; Anja Huber; Manuel Surek',year=2018,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.10351',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.10351',locator='Version 1, 28 February 2018; Section 5, Definition 11, Theorem 12 and Remark 13. The only arXiv revision listed at review time.'),dict(id='journal',title='Efficient Black-Box Reductions for Separable Cost Sharing',authors='Tobias Harks; Martin Hoefer; Anja Schedel; Manuel Surek',year=2020,url='https://doi.org/10.1287/moor.2020.1050',locator='Published online 25 August 2020; Mathematics of Operations Research 46(1), 134–158, February 2021. Publisher abstract and metadata checked; journal full text was not accessible during this review.')]
save(i,dict(title='Polynomial-time cost sharing with concave edge costs',formal='Is there a deterministic algorithm that, for every n-series-parallel connection game with nonnegative, nondecreasing, discrete-concave shareable edge costs and fixed player-specific delays, and every supplied path profile P, runs in time polynomial in the input bit length and outputs a path profile Q and a polynomial-time evaluable separable cost-sharing protocol ξ such that C(Q)≤C(P) and Q is a pure Nash equilibrium under ξ? The game, encoding, cost and equilibrium are defined below. This asks for the polynomial-time extension of the source’s fixed-cost reduction to its stated concave-cost class; it does not require a prescribed sequence of steps from the original algorithm.',definitions='There are n≥1 unweighted players and a finite undirected loopless multigraph G=(V,E), with parallel edges distinguished. Each player i has distinct connected terminals sᵢ,tᵢ and may choose any simple sᵢ–tᵢ path. The graph is irredundant: every vertex and edge lies on a permitted path of at least one player. For each i, let Gᵢ be the union of all its permitted paths. The n-series-parallel promise means Gᵢ can be obtained from a single edge with terminals sᵢ,tᵢ by repeatedly subdividing an edge into two edges through a new vertex, or adding an edge parallel to an existing edge, preserving the two terminals. This promise concerns each player’s path subgraph; treewidth at most two for G alone is insufficient. For each edge e the input lists rational values cₑ(0),…,cₑ(n), with cₑ(0)=0 and increments cₑ(j)−cₑ(j−1) nonnegative and nonincreasing for j=1,…,n. The total shareable cost depends only on the number of users, not their identities or weights. The input also gives a nonnegative rational delay dᵢ,ₑ for each player and edge. Graph data, terminals and a supplied simple path Pᵢ for every player are explicit; rational numerators and positive denominators are encoded in binary. This finite-table convention makes the source’s polynomial-time question precise; it imposes no bound on the magnitudes of the costs. For a profile R, set Nₑ(R)={i:e∈Rᵢ} and C(R)=∑ₑcₑ(|Nₑ(R)|)+∑ᵢ∑ₑ∈Rᵢdᵢ,ₑ. A protocol gives nonnegative rational shares ξᵢ,ₑ(S) for user sets S⊆{1,…,n}. Shares are zero for i∉S and sum over i∈S to cₑ(|S|). A share depends on the edge and its user set; it may also use the fixed game data and the computed output, but cannot depend on choices made on other edges. Player i’s private cost at R is πᵢ(R)=∑ₑ∈Rᵢ(ξᵢ,ₑ(Nₑ(R))+dᵢ,ₑ). A pure Nash equilibrium Q satisfies πᵢ(Q)≤πᵢ(Q₋ᵢ,Rᵢ) for every i and every permitted alternate path Rᵢ. The protocol must be stable for this game, meaning some pure equilibrium exists; the returned Q witnesses this. The algorithm returns polynomially many bits describing ξ, with a fixed deterministic evaluator that, given the game, this description, i,e and the n-bit indicator of S, returns the exact share in polynomial time. It need not list all 2ⁿ user sets. Both construction and evaluation have uniform polynomial bounds in the stated bit length; no approximation of budget balance, equilibrium or C(Q)≤C(P) is allowed.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove or refute existence of an algorithm and evaluator meeting all guarantees for every promised graph, concave cost table, delay table and initial profile. A positive answer must bound bit complexity, including exact cost-share representation, and establish the equilibrium for all unilateral path deviations. Termination without a polynomial bound, a result for fixed costs alone, or an approximate equilibrium does not meet the criterion. A lower bound under a complexity assumption must state that assumption explicitly.',context_blocks=[dict(title='From an allocation to an equilibrium',text='The reduction receives any feasible allocation and must preserve or lower its social cost while making its output stable through budget-balanced local shares. Thus an α-approximation for the underlying allocation problem yields a protocol whose best equilibrium costs at most α times the social optimum; this is the price-of-stability connection.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The missing extension is concavity',text='Theorem 10 establishes polynomial time for fixed shareable costs, meaning cₑ(k) is constant for all k≥1. The following remark extends the existence and cost-preservation statements to nondecreasing discrete-concave costs but explicitly leaves polynomial time unknown. The full 2018 version repeats this as Remark 13.',citation='full'),dict(title='The graph and strategy classes cannot be enlarged silently',text='The source shows that generalized series-parallel graphs can have an optimal allocation that no separable protocol enforces, even with fixed costs and no delays. Its polynomial-time result for matroid strategy sets with more general costs addresses a different family of feasible allocations.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Scope of the later-version check',text='The work appeared online in Mathematics of Operations Research in 2020 and in its 2021 volume. The publisher abstract still describes the connection-game result with fixed costs. This review checked that abstract and publication metadata, but could not inspect the journal’s full concave-cost remark.',citation='journal')],why='Tests whether economies of scale can be combined with efficiently computed, exactly stable local cost allocations on a graph class that already supports the required existence theorem. A polynomial reduction would transfer algorithmic welfare guarantees to strategic network formation.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2018',text='Theorem 10 proves an efficient fixed-cost reduction on n-series-parallel networks with player-specific delays. Its following remark leaves the concave-cost running-time guarantee open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2020-08-25',text='Journal version published online; the publisher’s fixed-cost abstract and bibliographic metadata were checked, without access to its full text.',citation='journal')],status='open',status_note='Recovered the graph, user-count cost and private-delay restrictions from the primary conference text and full arXiv version. Both state the concave running-time question. Checked the 2020/2021 journal metadata and abstract and searched subsequent work and the authors’ publication lists; no resolution matching this extension was found. Journal full text was inaccessible, so this is a source-grounded open question with a limited later-version check, not a proof that no later solution exists.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Connects polynomial-time social-cost approximation to exact equilibrium implementation in networks with economies of scale; the required existence theorem is already available. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the specific concave-cost extension on n-series-parallel networks, defined a finite rational encoding and exact output protocol, and distinguished other graph and strategy classes.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['Players choose terminal-connecting paths and split the edge costs while paying their own fixed delays.','On the source’s n-series-parallel networks, a low-cost allocation can be transformed into an equilibrium without increasing social cost.','For fixed edge costs this transformation is polynomial-time; for concave costs the source leaves its running time open.','The card asks for a polynomial-time transformation with exact budget balance and an efficiently evaluable local sharing rule.','The full 2018 source was checked; the later journal abstract was accessible, but its full text was not.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','journal'])),notes=['Recovered n-series-parallel path-subgraph promise, unweighted user-count concavity and player-specific fixed delays.','Specified rational finite-table input and compact exactly evaluable protocol, preserving arbitrary initial-profile cost guarantee.','Interpreted the source as existence of a polynomial implementation of its extension, not a required trace of a particular algorithm.','Checked full2018 remark and later journal metadata/abstract; recorded inaccessible full journal text.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://www.algo.rwth-aachen.de/team/hoefer/publications.shtml','https://www.fim.uni-passau.de/en/mathopt/publications/journal-articles'])
